<p>What follows is reproduced in John's own words, exactly as he wrote them: nothing edited, softened, or left out. It carries the same broad, joking, tall-tale narrator he uses throughout "Please Go to Hell" and his other books, a folksy small-town-goofball persona that was a deliberate comic choice, not a reflection of how he actually wrote or spoke. One story in Chapter I turns on a boyhood nickname that is a racial slur; the book prints it without explanation and it is kept as printed, in the story's title and text, because changing it would misrepresent what John wrote. The scans were read twice by machine and the doubtful words checked against the page images. Where a slip appears below, it is a slip in the printed book itself, and it has been kept as printed. Numbers in parentheses are the book's own printed page numbers.</p>

<p>The cover prints the title as two words, "Thunder Mug," with the subtitle "The Preacher's Comin'"; the book's running text and its table of contents print it as one, "Thundermug." Both are kept as printed. The Introduction calls this "the third publication," after "Once Around the Thunder Mug" and "Who Upset the Thundermug."</p>

