<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="acknowledgements">Acknowledgements</h2>

  <p>This is the hardest page in the publication to write. What it boils down to is, "Who in the Hell am I going to embarrass this time." I'd like to include my Texas friends, the Britains, Johnsons, McDonoughs, Stutzmans, Garrisons and Thompsons, but they are all good shots and I'm a sitting duck on this side of the valley. They are proud, patriotic, hard workers and friendly. That's a combination you can't beat with a stick and they appreciate humor. Just how much humor is the question that concerns me.</p>

  <p>Clarence "Frosty" Snow led me astray a couple of times and deserves to be embarrassed, Everette Wyatt engaged me in a drinking match in 1947 that left us both in deep trouble. Everette married Jackie Moore, my girlfriend in the '30s, and she wasn't pleased at his condition after we bid each other goodnight. Darn it all, Jackie, I was just as sick.</p>

  <p>The names of those who deserve to be embarrassed are just too lengthy for this page. Perhaps Genevieve Sullivan, Cherie Larson, Emma Mae Wilson, Charlotte Lane, Mary Johnson, Edna Ruth Van Aken, Ralph Snow and Bernard Shaw will forgive me. They were students of Professor E. D. Watson who is included in one of the stories. Maybe they will remember that he was an excellent teacher and drove his rag tag ball team to valley towns at his own expense.</p>

  <p>The most appropriate acknowledgement with little fear of consequence would be to thank Nicholas C. Creede, the intrepid prospector who braved the elements and untold hardship of a harsh environment to discover the "Holy Moses" silver mine which led to one of the richest mining districts in the State of Colorado and brought our Grandfathers and Grandmothers to a tent city that would later bear the name "Creede."</p>

  <p>Thank you, Nicholas C. Creede. Without you we might have been raising tobacco in North Carolina or switching rail-cars in Chicago. Your knowledge and fortitude made it possible for us to be raised in the finest environment and among the finest of people.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>"Once Around the Thunder Mug" and "Who Upset the Thundermug" both close their own front matter with the same thanks to Nicholas C. Creede; see <a href="/writings/who-upset-the-thundermug/#acknowledgements">that page</a>. E. D. Watson is the school superintendent of <a href="/writings/get-grandpa-off-the-thunder-mug/chapter-iii/#bearly-heroes">"Bearly Heroes"</a>, below.</em></p>
</section>

