<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="foreword">Foreword</h2>

  <p>There was a reason for publishing the Thundermug stories, but I can't remember whether it was vanity or the hope for monetary reward. In all probability sleepless nights and long winters aided and abetted the crime.</p>

  <p>This effort should be the last. I am beginning to believe the stories and if I am foolish enough to believe them, most assuredly, others will follow suit. That could create problems I am not prepared to handle.</p>

  <p>There are a lot of humorous stories locked up in the memories of people who are reluctant to write due to fear of embarrassment or ridicule. I nearly trashed the first manuscript thinking I was doing nothing but making a darned fool of myself. I did make a darned fool of myself, but I sort of liked the attention. For the bulk of my life I tried to maintain a posture that would be compatible with society and receive approval of my relatives. All that time there was a darned fool, just under the skin, waiting to emerge and be recognized.</p>

  <p>It will have been worth all the inner turmoil, time and effort, if the Thundermug books provide one or more of you, out there, to think... "Hey, if that crazy old Jackson can get his stories published, maybe I can too." And you can!</p>

  <p>In this era of high technology where our colleges are cranking out professionals faster than the government can make room for them there is a whole new frontier opening up for darned fools. The line forms to the write.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>"This effort should be the last" did not hold: a fourth book, "Third Stool from the Rear," and a fifth, "Hell Cats And She Ghosts," followed. See <a href="/people/john-richard-jackson/">John's page</a> for the full list of his books.</em></p>
</section>

