<p class="source-doc-note"><em>Caption, in full: "The year was 1934 and director Thomas Drysdale led his award winning Creede Schools Band in a rousing 'Happy Birthday' to Senator Lawrence Phipps followed by a selection of marches. The young lady under Mr. Drysdale's baton is Margaret McCrone (Lamb). To her right Bob Manning plays the flute, to his right Raymond Lane, Mary Slaght and Eleanor Johnson (Lehman) play saxophones. The highlight of the early Depression years was the annual birthday party for our Senator at his La Garita Ranch, 7 miles southeast of Creede. The entire town was invited to share in the event which included foot races and baseball games in addition to the stirring band music. There were tubs filled with ice and Nehi soda and food to fill the bellies of all who attended. After the festivities cars could be seen stopped on the roadway from the ranch to Creede so little shavers could relieve themselves. Band members not identified were: Tommy Drysdale, Ivan Van Aken, Charles Slaght, Edna Cossitt, Dorothy Workman, Rusty Ainsworth, Jimmy Workman, Irma Slaght, Jimmy Burkholder, Raymond Ball, Billy Matzen, Gordon Duncan, Alyce Turnipseed, Clinton Fairchild, Tommy Nance, Arlene Filley, Jimmy Barber, Frankie Davis, Joey Jackson, Bruce Barksdale, May Birdsey, Cloy Duncan and Arthur Weaver." "Frankie Davis" is <a href="/people/anna-maud-woodruff/#john-franklin-davis">Frank Davis's</a> son, John's first cousin, per family knowledge.</em></p>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="introduction">Introduction</h2>

  <p>The "Great Depression" of the 1930s was a rough period, anyone who lived through it will agree to that, but it was a grand era of humor and humorists. People of all races and creeds could laugh at each other without feeling guilty or offended because they were all in the same boat. For many, a good sense of humor was all that pulled them through daily agonies and frustrations as they worked at any job available and tried to feed their families on very meager incomes.</p>

  <p>The near tragic is often the most humorous. Like the time my Father lost his footing as we were re-shingling the roof of his house. He clipped the catwalk with his toes which vaulted him into space, backwards. I thought for two or three seconds he would be the first human to fly without mechanical help as his arms flailed like windmills in a vain effort to defy the law of gravity. I was still on the roof but I could see his stunned frame trying to recover and his shattered glasses resting nearby. He was alive. I began laughing, more in relief than anything else. "Laugh, you damned idiot" he moaned, "you'd laugh if it had killed me." I still laugh when I think about it and had it killed him it would have been a more merciful death than his bout with lung cancer that destroyed him, inch by inch, a decade later.</p>

  <p>The real humor, the humor that sticks with us, is an event or circumstance we can relate to that tickles us where we need it be tickled. It is something that rests in our memories like hot embers in a camp fire. All we need do is fan them a little to feel the warmth.</p>

  <p>The following stories were not designed to carry a plot or create characters. They are dredgings from The Thundermug as well as other stories that were not included in the weekly column for various reasons. Many of the stories were told to me and I accepted them to be basically true. Other stories are part and parcel of my misspent life. We hope you will find part or parts you can relate to, that will bring you a measure of the enjoyment we have had in bringing them to you.</p>

  <p>After the second publication (Who Upset the Thundermug) I was warned that I was being too harsh with our female counter parts which would invoke the wrath of feminist activists. I wasn't aware that such a group could read and hadn't considered I was in peril.</p>

  <p>I am reminded of a gathering in the Congregational Church Parsonage many years ago. It was a friendly group discussing topics of the day under the direction of our Reverend Mary Grubbs. As the meeting was nearing an end Reverend Mary stated, "In truth, there is little difference between man and woman." My old and faithful friend, Charles Steele, instantly replied, "Praise the Lord for that small difference."</p>

  <p>God bless you, dear ladies, but let us always remember that there is a difference between man and woman and the destiny of all mankind depends on that difference.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>John names no brother here and gives no date for the roof fall. "Echoes from the Thundermug" gives his father's death as 1957; "a decade later" would place the fall in the late 1940s, but the book gives no other date and the passage is kept exactly as printed, as an inference only. This is the first appearance in these books of the phrase "the second publication," which is how John describes "Who Upset the Thundermug" here.</em></p>
</section>

